{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"NARROWING (P0): the 15 minutes is compact-on-close, and swan DID emit its COMPACT.\n\n## Correction to the forensics\nSwan's compaction did not \"produce nothing to commit\". Commit `72ff0358f7`,\n`ticket: COMPACT 271c-e88f-3220-4c9b`, landed at **20:49:45** — 59 files changed (58 event\nfiles renamed to `.retired` + 1 SNAPSHOT). So the span is bounded on both ends:\n\n    20:35:49  SIGNATURE 271c            <- close write complete, store lock released\n    20:35:52  .ticket-write.lock.d created (pid 61327)  <- _compact_on_close re-acquires\n    20:49:45  COMPACT 271c committed    <- compaction done, lock released\n\n**13m53s, one continuous hold, owned entirely by `_compact_on_close`.** Of the three suspects,\n(a) compaction is the owner; (b) the best-effort push is NOT under the lock\n(`push_after_commit` docstring: \"Call AFTER the locked commit has released the store lock —\nthe push runs its own fetch/merge and must not nest inside the write lock\", and it takes no\nlock itself); (c) the atomic-staging change 61b49deaa6 touches `event_append`'s\n`stage_and_commit`/`batch_stage_and_commit`, which `_compact_locked` does not use — it does its\nown rename + `_git(add/commit)`.\n\n## Why compaction can hold the store lock for a quarter of an hour\nRaw git churn is NOT the cost. Measured on this tracker (54,931 tracked files, 745M):\n`git status --porcelain` 0.185s, `git write-tree` 0.014s. The cost is a **nested lock plus a\nlarge retry budget, taken while the store write lock is held**:\n\n`_compact_locked` acquires `lock.acquire(tracker, timeout=30, attempts=2)` and then runs every\ngit op through `compact_txn._git` -> `gitutil.run_git_write` -> `_with_index_lock_retry`, which\nopens with `_store_git_op_lock(tracker)` — a SECOND per-store advisory flock\n(`.git/rebar-git-op.lock`). Budgets, all inside the held store lock:\n  - `_STORE_GIT_LOCK_WAIT_S` = 30s waiting on the git-op lock (then degrades and proceeds);\n  - `_INDEX_LOCK_ATTEMPTS` = 10 attempts, jittered backoff capped at `_INDEX_LOCK_BACKOFF_CAP_S` 5s;\n  - each attempt is `_with_transient_head_retry` = up to `_TRANSIENT_HEAD_ATTEMPTS` 3 git children;\n  - each child bounded by `_LOCAL_GIT_TIMEOUT` = 120s.\nWorst case for ONE `git add`/`git commit` is therefore tens of minutes, and the store write\nlock has no ceiling of its own — `_compact_locked`'s 30s/2-attempt budget bounds only\n*acquiring* it, never *holding* it. Six agents were writing this store concurrently during the\nwindow, which is exactly the contention regime that walks that budget.\n\nThis is a hold-and-wait the lock module's own reasoning does not cover: `_store/lock.py`'s\ndocstring argues \"neither leg waits on the other across processes there is no hold-and-wait\ncycle => deadlock-free\", but that argument is about the fcntl and mkdir legs of the SAME lock.\nIt says nothing about a second advisory lock acquired *inside* the store lock, which is what\nevery lock-held git op in compaction does.\n\nAlso note the c2ba bound the coordinator asked about: compaction does NOT escape it —\n`run_git_write` applies the 120s watchdog — but 120s is a per-child bound multiplied by\n3 x 10 retries, so the bound does not constrain the aggregate hold.\n\n## Why the 7084 stand-aside does not save the close\n`_compact_on_close` probes `write_lock_is_busy` and skips when busy. For the closing process\nthat probe is structurally useless: the close released the store lock ~3s earlier (SIGNATURE\n20:35:49, probe ~20:35:52), so the store reads free and compaction takes the lock every time.\n\n## Fix shape (NOT yet implemented — needs a scope decision)\nThe lock must bracket only the final ref mutation: do the fold (parse, reduce, build SNAPSHOT)\noutside the store lock, then take the lock, re-check that no new events landed for the ticket\n(optimistic re-check, same TOCTOU discipline the ACs ask for — a change since the fold means\ndiscard this pass, not a stale write), rename + commit, release. Nested-lock waits and\nmulti-minute retry budgets must not sit inside the held store lock.\n\nThis changes WHEN compaction does its work relative to the close, which is a user-visible\nclose-semantics change beyond \"move verification outside the lock\" (already true), so I am\nholding for a scope decision rather than implementing unilaterally. The ticket is also\nunclaimed — no plan-review attestation — so implementing now would break claim-before-work.\nReady to proceed on either this ticket (re-pointed at the compaction holder) or\n`choosy-arthrodic-barbet` (cfcb-a023-09a0-42ad), whichever you prefer."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786593603956812001,"uuid":"bbf064fd-3677-4fd1-a285-84286d48e9b8"}